1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,233
Here's how most golden sets actually get built.

2
00:00:03,233 --> 00:00:08,202
Someone needs fifty examples by Thursday, so they open the database, sort by most

3
00:00:08,202 --> 00:00:10,533
recent, and copy the first fifty rows.

4
00:00:10,533 --> 00:00:15,685
Or worse, they remember the ten outputs that looked great in the demo and use those.

5
00:00:15,685 --> 00:00:20,776
Or an engineer gets asked for "a representative sample", which sounds rigorous and,

6
00:00:20,776 --> 00:00:22,985
underneath, just means random again.

7
00:00:22,985 --> 00:00:24,580
All three feel reasonable.

8
00:00:24,580 --> 00:00:28,567
Fifty is fifty, and a deadline doesn't care where they came from.

9
00:00:28,567 --> 00:00:32,247
But none of them does the actual job a golden set has to do.

10
00:00:32,247 --> 00:00:35,682
The right question was never how many examples you need.

11
00:00:35,682 --> 00:00:39,547
Fifty, five hundred, it doesn't matter if you chose them badly.

12
00:00:39,547 --> 00:00:44,515
The right question is which fifty, deliberately chosen, would actually catch this

13
00:00:44,515 --> 00:00:46,662
feature failing before a user does.

14
00:00:46,662 --> 00:00:49,484
That's a coverage decision, not a sample size.

15
00:00:49,484 --> 00:00:54,452
It's the difference between an instrument that tells you the truth and one that's

16
00:00:54,452 --> 00:00:56,722
been quietly built to agree with you.

17
00:00:57,272 --> 00:01:01,800
Here's why picking at random, even fifty of them, isn't good enough on its own.

18
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:05,354
Picture a failure mode that hits one real user in twenty-five.

19
00:01:05,354 --> 00:01:06,443
About four percent.

20
00:01:06,443 --> 00:01:06,959
Not rare.

21
00:01:06,959 --> 00:01:10,971
The kind of thing that would show up in your support queue most weeks.

22
00:01:10,971 --> 00:01:15,786
Sample fifty cases completely at random, and on average you'd expect to see it about

23
00:01:15,786 --> 00:01:16,130
twice.

24
00:01:16,130 --> 00:01:17,276
Average is the trap.

25
00:01:17,276 --> 00:01:21,976
Work out the actual odds and there's a better than one in eight chance your random

26
00:01:21,976 --> 00:01:23,982
fifty contains zero examples of it.

27
00:01:23,982 --> 00:01:25,186
Not underrepresented.

28
00:01:25,186 --> 00:01:25,473
Zero.

29
00:01:25,473 --> 00:01:30,115
Ship on that golden set and you'd score ninety-something percent, feel confident,

30
00:01:30,115 --> 00:01:34,586
and carry a blind spot the size of one user in twenty-five, sitting completely

31
00:01:34,586 --> 00:01:35,962
outside your instrument.

32
00:01:35,962 --> 00:01:37,509
Random doesn't fail loudly.

33
00:01:37,509 --> 00:01:39,458
It fails silently, which is worse.

34
00:01:39,458 --> 00:01:41,120
And this is the mild version.

35
00:01:41,120 --> 00:01:45,477
Real golden sets aren't built from one clean random draw, they're built from

36
00:01:45,477 --> 00:01:50,062
whatever transcripts happened to be handy, which is biased before you even start

37
00:01:50,062 --> 00:01:50,578
counting.

38
00:01:51,128 --> 00:01:55,941
So here's the deliberate version: four buckets, and fifty split across them on

39
00:01:55,941 --> 00:01:59,088
purpose, not fifty of whatever was easiest to pull.

40
00:01:59,088 --> 00:02:03,285
Twenty are common path, the ordinary case your users hit constantly.

41
00:02:03,285 --> 00:02:08,407
Skimp here and you can pass your golden set while quietly regressing the thing that

42
00:02:08,407 --> 00:02:12,479
matters most, because nothing in your fifty was even measuring it.

43
00:02:12,479 --> 00:02:17,293
Fifteen are known failure modes: the specific ways you've already watched this

44
00:02:17,293 --> 00:02:18,157
feature break.

45
00:02:18,157 --> 00:02:23,093
The ticket-summary case from Section 0, the one that dropped a deadline, belongs

46
00:02:23,093 --> 00:02:23,402
here.

47
00:02:23,402 --> 00:02:27,660
Every one of these is a bug you're refusing to let back in unnoticed.

48
00:02:27,660 --> 00:02:32,720
Ten are edge cases: real, unusual inputs, a ticket in a second language, a summary

49
00:02:32,720 --> 00:02:35,065
of almost nothing, a field left blank.

50
00:02:35,065 --> 00:02:39,385
Rare, but they happen, and it's exactly where AI features get strange.

51
00:02:39,385 --> 00:02:44,506
And five are adversarial: someone deliberately trying to break the feature, or bend

52
00:02:44,506 --> 00:02:49,505
it into doing something it shouldn't, a prompt engineered to leak instructions, a

53
00:02:49,505 --> 00:02:53,516
request dressed up to extract data it has no business giving out.

54
00:02:54,066 --> 00:02:57,998
Here's what actually doing this looks like on a Monday morning.

55
00:02:57,998 --> 00:03:03,115
You start from real production logs or pilot transcripts, never invented examples.

56
00:03:03,115 --> 00:03:08,170
An invented example only ever tests what you already imagined, which is precisely

57
00:03:08,170 --> 00:03:09,980
not the point of any of this.

58
00:03:09,980 --> 00:03:14,661
Filter that raw traffic into rough candidates for each of the four buckets.

59
00:03:14,661 --> 00:03:19,841
Then a human, you, or whoever owns this feature, actually reads every candidate and

60
00:03:19,841 --> 00:03:24,834
tags it: common path, known failure, edge case, or adversarial, with one line on

61
00:03:24,834 --> 00:03:25,083
why.

62
00:03:25,083 --> 00:03:30,076
That tagging step is the one people try to skip, and it's the one that makes the

63
00:03:30,076 --> 00:03:31,699
other three mean anything.

64
00:03:31,699 --> 00:03:35,568
Curate down to fifty, dropping near-duplicates, and freeze it.

65
00:03:35,568 --> 00:03:40,748
Version it the way you'd version code: a date, a change log, one owner who approves

66
00:03:40,748 --> 00:03:41,122
edits.

67
00:03:41,122 --> 00:03:46,177
The moment it's editable on a whim, it stops being an instrument and goes back to

68
00:03:46,177 --> 00:03:48,923
being an opinion, just a more expensive one.

69
00:03:49,473 --> 00:03:50,746
Say the cost plainly.

70
00:03:50,746 --> 00:03:55,835
Budget a real afternoon for this, not the twenty minutes it looks like on a roadmap.

71
00:03:55,835 --> 00:04:00,621
Reading two hundred transcripts and arguing about four borderline ones is slow,

72
00:04:00,621 --> 00:04:05,286
unglamorous work, and it happens before you've built anything anyone can see.

73
00:04:05,286 --> 00:04:10,254
Nobody puts "read transcripts for three hours" on a roadmap, so it quietly doesn't

74
00:04:10,254 --> 00:04:14,132
happen, and the golden set ends up built the lazy way after all.

75
00:04:14,132 --> 00:04:18,978
Teams that skip straight to the eval harness later in this section end up with a

76
00:04:18,978 --> 00:04:24,007
beautifully engineered instrument pointed at fifty cases that don't prove anything.

77
00:04:24,007 --> 00:04:26,188
The harness was never the hard part.

78
00:04:26,188 --> 00:04:29,702
This was, and it still is, every time you refresh the set.

79
00:04:30,252 --> 00:04:35,409
Notice what the definition insists on: deliberately covering all four, not fifty of

80
00:04:35,409 --> 00:04:37,210
whatever was easiest to pull.

81
00:04:37,210 --> 00:04:41,311
Miss a bucket entirely and you haven't built a smaller golden set.

82
00:04:41,311 --> 00:04:44,914
You've built a blind spot with a pass rate attached to it.

83
00:04:44,914 --> 00:04:49,139
The exercise is how you find that blind spot before production does.

84
00:04:49,139 --> 00:04:53,239
Two hundred transcripts, sorted by hand, takes about an afternoon.

85
00:04:53,239 --> 00:04:58,333
Most people who do this for the first time get an uncomfortable result: one bucket

86
00:04:58,333 --> 00:05:01,999
is nearly empty, usually adversarial, sometimes edge cases.

87
00:05:01,999 --> 00:05:03,925
That gap is not a data problem.

88
00:05:03,925 --> 00:05:08,647
It's the actual shape of what your current testing has never once looked at.

89
00:05:08,647 --> 00:05:11,939
Fill it from real transcripts if you can find enough.

90
00:05:11,939 --> 00:05:17,034
Where you genuinely can't, because nobody has tried to attack the feature yet, say

91
00:05:17,034 --> 00:05:21,320
so in the spec instead of quietly padding the bucket with lookalikes.

92
00:05:21,320 --> 00:05:26,477
Which would you rather defend in a launch review: an empty bucket, or one padded to

93
00:05:26,477 --> 00:05:27,098
look full?

94
00:05:27,098 --> 00:05:28,900
An honest gap is information.

95
00:05:28,900 --> 00:05:31,509
A padded one is a false sense of coverage.

96
00:05:32,059 --> 00:05:33,847
One thing before you move on.

97
00:05:33,847 --> 00:05:37,483
Download the golden dataset spec, attached to this lecture.

98
00:05:37,483 --> 00:05:42,537
It has the four buckets built in, with space for a count and a one-line reason for

99
00:05:42,537 --> 00:05:43,708
every case you add.

100
00:05:43,708 --> 00:05:48,700
Then do the sort while the categories are fresh: pull your two hundred, tag them,

101
00:05:48,700 --> 00:05:49,933
and see what's thin.

102
00:05:49,933 --> 00:05:54,555
Most people find out something uncomfortable within the first thirty cases.

103
00:05:54,555 --> 00:05:59,609
Next lecture, we put a rubric underneath all fifty, so "good" means the same thing

104
00:05:59,609 --> 00:06:03,307
every time one gets scored, no matter who's holding the pen.
